WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.568
For two episodes, the dial and the
sliders have simply known where to stop.

00:00:04.608 --> 00:00:08.883
Four sliders settled, the dial
settled, and we read off the answers.

00:00:09.000 --> 00:00:11.715
But nobody handed them those answers.

00:00:11.755 --> 00:00:16.497
All they had was the data: 103
patients, and who came back.

00:00:16.633 --> 00:00:20.710
So what makes one setting of the
dial and sliders better than another?

00:00:20.750 --> 00:00:22.850
And how do they find the best one?

00:00:24.433 --> 00:00:26.654
Welcome back to Model in Motion.

00:00:26.694 --> 00:00:28.751
Today: fitting by balance.

00:00:30.833 --> 00:00:34.461
Start with any setting at
all. Here is the plainest:

00:00:34.501 --> 00:00:39.012
every patient, at every clinic,
gets the overall rate, 41%.

00:00:39.133 --> 00:00:44.832
Now ask of each patient: what probability did
this guess give to what actually happened?

00:00:44.872 --> 00:00:51.046
For a patient who was readmitted,
41%. For one who wasn't, 59%.

00:00:51.167 --> 00:00:53.493
Multiply those 103 numbers together,

00:00:53.533 --> 00:00:59.532
and you have the probability the guess gave
to the whole story. That is its likelihood.

00:00:59.572 --> 00:01:04.100
It is tiny: a decimal point
followed by thirty zeros.

00:01:04.140 --> 00:01:08.370
So we take its logarithm instead: minus 69.6.

00:01:08.500 --> 00:01:12.172
Think of that number as a height.
A better guess stands higher.

00:01:12.212 --> 00:01:17.255
The height itself means nothing. Only
the climb from one guess to another does.

00:01:18.833 --> 00:01:22.485
So which way is up? Bring
back the meter from Episode 3:

00:01:22.525 --> 00:01:25.705
observed on one side, expected on the other.

00:01:25.745 --> 00:01:29.039
Here is one for every clinic,
under the plain guess.

00:01:29.167 --> 00:01:33.356
Dune expected 4.9 readmissions and saw 7.

00:01:33.396 --> 00:01:40.141
Birch expected 12.2 and saw 14.
Elm expected 4.5 and saw none.

00:01:40.267 --> 00:01:43.256
And here is the fact that
makes fitting possible.

00:01:43.296 --> 00:01:48.078
Each meter's tilt is exactly the slope
of the height in that clinic's gamma.

00:01:48.118 --> 00:01:53.225
Observed above expected: push the
slider up. Observed below: pull it down.

00:01:55.800 --> 00:02:01.499
Try it with Dune. Push its slider up, and
among the clinics, only Dune's meter responds.

00:02:01.539 --> 00:02:06.070
Its expected count climbs toward
7, and the height rises with it.

00:02:06.200 --> 00:02:10.960
When the meter is level, the height is
as high as this one slider can take it.

00:02:11.000 --> 00:02:14.678
Push further, and the meter tips
the other way. The height falls.

00:02:14.800 --> 00:02:19.344
The dial has a meter too. It weighs
only the higher-risk patients:

00:02:19.384 --> 00:02:24.665
29 of them were readmitted, and the
plain guess expected fewer than 19.

00:02:24.800 --> 00:02:27.640
Turn the dial up until that meter is level.

00:02:27.680 --> 00:02:32.190
But look at the clinics. Every one that
has higher-risk patients has shifted.

00:02:32.333 --> 00:02:35.117
So nothing can be settled one piece at a time.

00:02:35.157 --> 00:02:39.190
The dial and the sliders have
to find their places together.

00:02:41.767 --> 00:02:45.077
And here is where they stop. The dial at 2.02,

00:02:45.117 --> 00:02:49.258
and the sliders where we
found them two episodes ago.

00:02:49.298 --> 00:02:52.698
Four clinic meters are
level, and so is the dial's.

00:02:52.833 --> 00:02:59.406
In each of those four clinics, expected
now equals observed: 13, 14, 8 and 7.

00:02:59.446 --> 00:03:02.015
That is what fitting is: balancing.

00:03:02.133 --> 00:03:07.896
One detail. The balance holds for each
clinic's total, not for each group inside it.

00:03:07.936 --> 00:03:15.095
Alder expected 8.2 among its lower-risk
patients and 4.8 among its higher-risk ones.

00:03:15.135 --> 00:03:18.056
Not 8 and 5. But together, 13.

00:03:18.200 --> 00:03:21.895
And Elm? Its meter can never
level. With no readmissions,

00:03:21.935 --> 00:03:25.819
expected stays above observed
wherever the slider sits.

00:03:25.859 --> 00:03:29.661
So the slider keeps sliding, gaining
a little less height each time.

00:03:29.701 --> 00:03:31.350
We'll come back to Elm.

00:03:31.500 --> 00:03:34.933
With Elm's slider run all the way down,

00:03:34.973 --> 00:03:40.042
the height has climbed from
minus 69.6 to minus 55.6.

00:03:41.633 --> 00:03:44.486
One last picture. For
each position of the dial,

00:03:44.526 --> 00:03:47.828
level the clinics' meters,
and record the height.

00:03:47.967 --> 00:03:51.938
The result is a hill, with one top, at 2.02.

00:03:51.978 --> 00:03:58.216
The pooled step from Episode 3, 1.75,
sits a little way down the left side.

00:03:58.333 --> 00:04:00.875
And notice how gently the top is rounded.

00:04:00.915 --> 00:04:05.168
That gentleness is the roughness
we keep promising to measure.

00:04:06.767 --> 00:04:08.263
Let's gather it up.

00:04:08.303 --> 00:04:12.572
A guess is scored by the probability
it gave to what actually happened.

00:04:12.612 --> 00:04:18.034
Each clinic's meter, observed minus expected,
says which way its slider should move.

00:04:18.074 --> 00:04:21.666
And at the best fit, every
meter that can level is level.

00:04:21.800 --> 00:04:25.728
A caution. Level meters do not
show that the model is right.

00:04:25.768 --> 00:04:28.991
Any model with a number for
each clinic will level them.

00:04:29.031 --> 00:04:33.926
Whether it is right has to be judged
inside the clinics, and that comes later.

00:04:34.067 --> 00:04:39.168
We have seen where the top of the hill is,
not how to get there. No formula gives it.

00:04:39.208 --> 00:04:41.414
Next time: climbing the hill.

00:04:41.533 --> 00:04:45.638
This has been Model in Motion.
Thanks for following the story.
